KotlinSwiftFlutter OkHttpManual (any client, KMP) <p>Wrap your <code>WebSocketListener</code> to log every incoming frame, and send with <code>sendWithFlocon</code> to log the outgoing ones. The <code>id</code> identifies the connection, and is what lets you inject messages into it (see below).</p> <pre><code>val request = Request.Builder()
    .url("wss://your-websocket-endpoint.com")
    .build()

val myListener = object : WebSocketListener() {
    override fun onMessage(webSocket: WebSocket, text: String) {
        // your handler
    }
}

val webSocket = client.newWebSocket(
    request,
    myListener.listenWithFlocon(id = "wss://your-websocket-endpoint.com"),
)

// sends the message and logs it
webSocket.sendWithFlocon("hello")
</code></pre> <p>Imports come from <code>io.github.openflocon.flocon.okhttp.websocket</code>.</p> <p>For another WebSocket client, forward the events to the network plugin:</p> <pre><code>Flocon.networkPlugin.logWebSocket(
    FloconWebSocketEvent(
        websocketUrl = "wss://api.example.com/ws",
        event = FloconWebSocketEvent.Event.SendMessage,
        message = outgoingText,
    )
)

Flocon.networkPlugin.logWebSocket(
    FloconWebSocketEvent(
        websocketUrl = "wss://api.example.com/ws",
        event = FloconWebSocketEvent.Event.ReceiveMessage,
        message = incomingText,
    )
)
</code></pre> <p>The other events are <code>Open</code>, <code>Closing</code>, <code>Closed</code> and <code>Error</code> (pass <code>error = throwable</code>).</p> <p>WebSocket traffic is not intercepted automatically. Log the events of the connections you want to inspect:</p> <pre><code>let task = URLSession.shared.webSocketTask(with: url)
